<figure><img sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" data-recalc-dims="1" height="778" width="1360" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Glenn Fleishman, art by Shafer Brown" /></figure>
<p>Apple added natural-language entry in Calendar and Reminders a few versions ago so you could loosely describe an event or task and have the app do the work. To use natural-language entry, you invoked a Quick Event/Quick Task entry field by tapping or clicking the + button at the top-left or top-right corner of the apps, then writing your stilted description.</p>
<p>This natural-language approach was better than the tedium of—horrible as it sounds—selecting items from pop-up menus and entering values. The accuracy was a little scattershot, though, so I wasn&#8217;t always keen on using it, as I thought the average time it took to correct mistaken inferences was greater than the time saved.</p>
<p>Now in iOS 27, iPadOS 27, and macOS 27, Calendar and Reminders gain Apple Intelligence for new events and tasks. The + icon is still there, but it brings up a complete entry dialog (with Event and Reminder tabs at the top). It&#8217;s a lot better for events, but is quite weak on reminder details.</p>

<h2>Create a new Smart Event</h2>
<p>Apple calls this new style of entry a Smart Event. You can use this method when you add an event or task using any of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Click or tap the New Event or Reminder (+) icon:</strong> In any view but Day, click the New Event (+) icon in the upper-right corner of Calendar on an iPhone and upper-left corner (just to the right of the sidebar, if visible) on a Mac or iPad. This action opens a new event/reminder sheet with defaults for most settings. The time is set to a one-hour duration from the start of the current hour (i.e., if it’s 2:33 p.m., the event is preset to 2 p.m. to 3 p.m.). The day is either the current day, or the day for an event you have selected when you tap or click.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Double-click or click and drag in the calendar (Mac in Day or Week view):</strong> Double-click to create an event with a default duration at the point you clicked; click and drag to control the duration starting at the click point.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Double-click to the left of the day numeral (Mac in Month view):</strong> Double-clicking in the space to the left of the numeral for the day in the Month view brings up an entry prefilled as 9 a.m. to 10 a.m. for that date.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Touch and hold in the calendar (iPhone/iPad):</strong> Touch and hold to create a one-hour event.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>An Event pop-up dialog now appears with the details noted above filled in for time and date. The main field reads Title or Describe Event. (If you don&#8217;t have Apple Intelligence on your device updated to version 27, the field reads Title, and you get no natural-language or AI support.)</p>
<h2>Compose a calendar event or task</h2>
<p>For a calendar entry, you can type in a title or write a description of the event, including people, a place, a duration, and other details. Apple Intelligence parses it using context and past events to help flesh out the event details.</p>
<p>It’s not the kind of natural language you might expect from a chatbot, where you could say something like, “I’d like you to have lunch on Thursday with my friend Joe at Oxbow.” Rather, you still have to use slightly more stilted language, such as <code>Lunch with Joe on Thursday at Oxbow</code>. It’s still easier!</p>
<p>As you type, Apple Intelligence highlights parts it recognizes in bold red type to indicate a match, or it marks them in bold gray if you need to tap to choose. For instance, in the figure, <code>Lunch</code>, <code>Joe,</code> and <code>Oxbow</code> appear in gray because it wasn’t sure when, who, or which location I meant. As I clicked each gray item, I could choose.</p>
<figure><img height="1146" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1500" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/composite-appint-calendar-illo-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Constructed graphic showing Calendar event creation with arrows pointing to pop-up menus and other details that can be changed. Typing &quot;Lunch with Joe on Thursday at Oxbow,&quot; is the main focus, with callouts examining lunch, Joe, Thursday, and Oxbow." /><figcaption>Smart Events in Calendar let you describe what an event is and then pick details that Apple Intelligence has highlighted.</figcaption></figure>
<p>You should note a few things in the figure:</p>
<ul>
<li>Apple Intelligence made the assumption that lunch is at 12:00 p.m., but didn’t set the event to that time.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>It interpreted <code>Thursday</code> as the day I was making the appointment (the same day), but I couldn’t eat lunch at noon since it was already past noon. Hence the lunchtime confusion.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxXp2szmUk4">Every Joe I know</a> appeared in the list, ordered roughly by probability based on frequency of contact, I think.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Locations were listed in order of proximity. I don’t know why the same Oxbow appeared twice.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Apple Intelligence performs much more poorly with reminders. It picks up date and time, as well as a description of a repeating schedule, like <code>every other week</code>. But, in my testing, Apple Intelligence wouldn&#8217;t explicitly fill in most Reminders fields from the description: it wouldn&#8217;t set a List, Priority, Flag, etc.</p>
<h2>Revise an existing Calendar event</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img height="410" sizes="(max-width: 750px) calc(100vw - 60px), (max-width: 1010px) 45vw, 345px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="596" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/calendar-revised-entry-ai-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of a Siri AI calendar entry reading &quot;Meet Joe on Tuesday on Nov 3 from 11am to 12pm,&quot; with date and times highlighted in red. Below, parsed fields show the title, Nov 3, 2026, 11:00AM–12:00PM, and a 30-minute alert." /><figcaption>You can revise an entry and have the smartness appear.</figcaption></figure>
<p>As you compose your description, if you click on a field within the entry, the description changes to the inferred title, but leaves the original text you entered above it in smaller type with a Siri AI icon at its far left. You can tap the text to edit it, and your original text returns to the Title or Describe Event field.</p>
<p>If you&#8217;ve already created an entry, you can tap or double-click it to reveal its details, then enter a new description in the Title field and tap or click Edit Description above the Title field, which is also to the right of the Siri AI icon.</p>
<p>In both cases—editing a new entry or revising an existing one—the Apple Intelligence analysis reappears in the Title or Describe Event field with the same highlighting and clickability of items.</p>
<h2>Add a task, smartly</h2>
<p>The new Apple Intelligence method of describing a task doesn&#8217;t appear when you tap or click the New Task (+) icon in the Reminders app. Instead, you have to create or edit a task, then tap or click the info (i) icon to reveal the Details pane or view, and then use the Describe Reminder field. As with a calendar event, you tap or click Edit Description.</p>
